/-!
# The twist form of the graded ring map `ψ^d : S^(d) ⟶ Γ_*(U_d, 𝒪_{U_d}(d))`

Let `S = ⨁_{k ≥ 0} 𝒮 k` be a graded ring, `X = Proj 𝒮`, and `d ≥ 1`. On the degree `d` open
`U_d = ⋃_{f ∈ 𝒮 d} D₊(f)` the sections of the twists `𝒪(k)` form a graded ring under pointwise
multiplication of local fractions, and its `d`-th Veronese part

  `⨁_{n ≥ 0} Γ(U_d, 𝒪_{U_d}(nd))`

receives the `d`-th Veronese subring `S^(d) = ⨁_{n ≥ 0} 𝒮 (nd)` by restricting to `U_d` the
graded ring map `S ⟶ ⨁_k Γ(X, 𝒪_X(k))` on global sections: a homogeneous element of degree `nd`
gives a global section of `𝒪_X(nd)`, which restricts to a section over `U_d`. That restriction is
the twist form of the map `ψ^d`. Stacks Equation 01N6 has as target the graded ring
`Γ_*(U_d, 𝒪_{U_d}(d)) = ⨁_{n ≥ 0} Γ(U_d, 𝒪_{U_d}(d)^{⊗ n})` of [Stacks, Tag 01CV]; that map is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenPsiTensor.psiTensor`.

The `d`-th twist is invertible on `U_d`, with no quasi-compactness hypothesis: `U_d` is contained
in the locus where the ratios of homogeneous elements whose degrees differ by `d` trivialize every
`d`-divisible twist, so `U_d` is a good open in the sense of the invertible locus of `Proj`. That
is why the `n`-th graded piece above, the sections of `𝒪_{U_d}(nd)`, is the group of sections of
the `n`-th tensor power of the one invertible sheaf `𝒪_{U_d}(d)`, and the displayed graded ring is
the graded section ring of an invertible sheaf; see the implementation notes.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.twistSections` — `Γ(Y, 𝒪(k))` for an open
  `Y ⊆ Proj 𝒮`.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.veroneseTwistSections` — the degree `n` piece
  `Γ(Y, 𝒪(nd))` of the `d`-th Veronese section ring.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.veroneseGradedRing` — the graded commutative
  ring structure on that family, the product being pointwise multiplication of local fractions.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.veroneseSectionRing` and
  `veroneseSectionRingGrading` — the ring `⨁_n Γ(Y, 𝒪(nd))` and its homogeneous components.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.veroneseSection` and `veroneseSectionHom` —
  the section of `𝒪(nd)` over `Y` attached to a homogeneous element of degree `nd`.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.veroneseSumHom` — the ring map
  `⨁_n 𝒮 (nd) ⟶ Γ_*(Y, 𝒪_Y(d))` assembled from those degreewise maps.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.psi` — the graded ring homomorphism
  `S^(d) ⟶ ⨁_n Γ(Y, 𝒪(nd))`, the twist form of `ψ^d`. The map `ψ^d` of Equation 01N6, into the
  section ring of the tensor powers of `𝒪_{U_d}(d)`, is
  `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenPsiTensor.psiTensor`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.degreeOpen_le_ratioLocus` and
  `isGoodOpen_degreeOpen` — the degree `d` open is a good open for `d`, so every `d`-divisible
  twist is invertible on it.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.veroneseSectionRingGrading_gradedMonoid` — the
  homogeneous components of the target contain its unit and are closed under its product.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.psi_apply_homogeneous` — on a homogeneous
  element of Veronese degree `n` the map `ψ^d` is the restriction to `U_d` of the global section
  of `𝒪(nd)` attached to it, placed in the degree `n` summand.
* `Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjDegreeOpenSections.nonvanishingLocus_veroneseSection_inter` —
  **global generation in degree one.** The nonvanishing locus on `U_d` of the section attached to
  `f ∈ 𝒮 d` is `D₊(f) ∩ U_d`, and these cover `U_d`, so `𝒪_{U_d}(d)` is generated by the image of
  `ψ^d` in degree one.

## Implementation notes

The target is the honest direct sum of the section groups `Γ(Y, 𝒪(nd))`, graded by `n`, rather
than the direct sum of the section groups of the tensor powers of `𝒪_{U_d}(d)`. The two agree,
because `𝒪_{U_d}(d)` is invertible and the multiplication maps `𝒪(nd) ⊗ 𝒪(md) ⟶ 𝒪((n+m)d)`
restrict to isomorphisms on `U_d` — both clauses of `isGoodOpen_degreeOpen`. Phrasing the target
by the twists themselves keeps `ψ^d` a construction rather than a choice.

A coherent family of isomorphisms `𝒪_{U_d}(d)^{⊗ n} ≅ 𝒪_{U_d}(nd)`, built from the canonical
multiplication isomorphisms rather than chosen degree by degree, is
`Stacks.AlgebraicGeometry.ProjTwistTensorPowDegreeOpen.theta`, with `thetaNat` the same family
at the Veronese indexing.

The product is the pointwise product of local fractions,
`Stacks.ProjTwistMultiplication.twistSectionsMul`, which is defined over an arbitrary open and
in arbitrary integer degrees.

Every graded-monoid law is proved first, as an equation between sections of a single twist with
the degree moved by `cast`, and the graded ring structure is then a list of references to those
theorems; `veroneseCast_apply` says the transport is invisible pointwise. This is the discipline
of `StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/ProjTwistSectionRing/GradedRing.lean`, for the same reason: a
law stated as the heterogeneous equality of `GradedMonoid` cannot be proved pointwise without
first eliminating the degree equality.

`veroneseGradedRing` is a `def`, not an instance: the family it grades is a section group of a
sheaf on `Proj`, and registering a graded ring structure on it globally would stand in the way of
instance search for every such section group. Statements that need it open with a `letI`, which
has to be `letI` and not `let` so that the statement is literally the quantified equation.

`Stacks.Veronese.veroneseGradingGradedRing` is likewise a `def` and takes the positivity of `d`
explicitly, so `psi` takes `0 < d` as an argument: the decomposition of the Veronese subring into
the pieces `𝒮 (nd)` is what makes a ring map out of it, and that decomposition needs `n ↦ nd`
injective.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01N6](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01N6)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01N5](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01N5)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 01MP](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01MP)
